{
  // VEGA's cutscenes (#2467; format: docs/developer/CUTSCENES.md, design: synthesis §6.3/§6.5). Ids follow synthesis §5.3
  // (StoryCutsceneIds.Vega); the finale set pieces and the ending close the list (#2478). Listed in arc order — the Story
  // tab's "Watch again" keeps this order.
  //
  // The prologue "Awakening" (#2470) is the cold open of the game, bound as the pack's introCutscene: it plays once per
  // player (ledger story:vega_protocol:cut:prologue) for a newcomer to the story, never on a tutorial restart, and again
  // only from the Story tab. Captions sit in the frame — the narration as subtitles, VEGA in her speaker box — page by page
  // when "Story text: wait for me" is on; the camera moves on its own (a terrain-aware orbit around the player's ship, then
  // in close), cuts to the pilot's seat as the console wakes, and VEGA's box glitches in for her first words. B0 is her
  // speaker page (it counts as heard with the prologue, so VEGA never wakes twice) and the call to adventure is the last
  // page. replaceLineKeys keeps these lines out of the side panel while the scene is owed; a client without cutscenes1 gets
  // the same five pages as the old full-screen prologue (Kind 4). chainNext: when the story track starts in the same join
  // (no tutorial for the player, a veteran) "the story so far" or chapter 1's card follows at once; when the basic training
  // starts, its welcome card (tutorial/tut.welcome, #2473) follows at once — whichever the join queued next.
  //
  // The chapter scenes and beat cards (#2474, maintainer decision (a) "more and more prominent VEGA"): every chapter of the
  // arc opens with its scene — at the beat that starts the chapter, and once for the player's CURRENT chapter at their
  // story-track start (after "the story so far"). Chapter 1 is a title card (T2: thin letterbox, "Chapter n" over the
  // chapter's title and a sting — free input, nothing held); chapter 2 shows VEGA's memory of the star chart (T3: "It mapped
  // these stars"); the key moments are staged: B5 "The Guardian" in the sleeping-core diorama — a red glowing column in an
  // iron shell, the camera rising along it, with the anomaly's hum (T3, #2477; it was the open sky before), B9 "What You
  // Are" at the player's REAL heal tank (T3 — a client that finds no tank on this world plays it as a card), and B12 with
  // the Guardian system revealed on the star map (T4: the line from here to the violet star and VEGA's jump-generator line,
  // then the chapter title; a singleplayer world holds while the camera is taken, never in multiplayer). #2477 staged the
  // two former chapter cards too (synthesis §6.5 lists them as T3): chapter 4 "The Verdict" keeps the darkest line as words
  // on a calm picture — a living world in a space vista, in VEGA's memory tones — and chapter 6 "It Still Sleeps" drifts
  // across VEGA's memory of the star chart out to its rim, where something faint and red pulses (no marker, no name). On
  // the low quality presets the synthetic stages are skipped and those three scenes play as cards again. Chapter 1's card
  // shows no beat: when a track starts the world may not have crossed B1 yet — its beat card (b01) follows it at once when
  // it has. The other beats are beat cards: "VEGA remembers" over the beat's title, then VEGA's speaker box with the beat —
  // free input, the memory grade. Beats fire on score, not on a place, so every shot is place-neutral (the player's view,
  // the sky, the chart, a synthetic stage) or anchored to the player's own ship. Every caption reuses the beat's own text
  // (replaceLineKeys keeps it out of VEGA's panel; the Journal holds it either way) — no new lore. The Guardian reveal's
  // line comes back to the panel when the player skips before it showed (returnOnSkip): it is the one line that names the
  // jump generator.
